      { "speaker": "Varma", "native": "రెండు కాఫీలు. ఒకటి చక్కెర లేకుండా.", "roman": "reṇḍu kāphīlu. okaṭi chakkera lēkuṇḍā.", "en": "Two coffees. One without sugar." },
      { "speaker": "Waiter", "native": "దోసె ఎలా ఉంది సార్?", "roman": "dōse elā undi sār?", "en": "How's the dosa, sir?" },
      { "speaker": "Varma", "native": "చాలా బాగుంది! కానీ చట్నీ కొంచెం కారంగా ఉంది.", "roman": "chālā bāgundi! kānī chaṭnī konchem kāraṅgā undi.", "en": "Very good! But the chutney is a bit spicy." },
      { "speaker": "Waiter", "native": "ఇంకా సాంబారు కావాలా?", "roman": "iṅkā sāmbāru kāvālā?", "en": "Would you like more sambar?" },
      { "speaker": "Varma", "native": "వద్దు, చాలు. బిల్లు ఇవ్వండి.", "roman": "vaddu, chālu. billu ivvaṇḍi.", "en": "No, that's enough. The bill, please." },
      { "speaker": "Waiter", "native": "మొత్తం నూట ఇరవై రూపాయలు.", "roman": "mottam nūṭa iravai rūpāyalu.", "en": "That's 120 rupees in total." }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "*భోజనం చేశారా?* ('Have you eaten?') is as much a greeting as a question, and a guest will be urged to eat more than once; saying *చాలు* or *వద్దండి* firmly (often with a hand over the plate) is how you stop the second and third helpings. Traditional meals are served on a banana leaf and eaten with the right hand. Telugu cuisine is known for *gongura* (sorrel) pickle, *pesarattu* (green-gram dosa) and Hyderabadi *biryani*.",
